WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.373
[MUSIC]

00:00:01.373 --> 00:00:05.178
Some of the biggest communication
challenges we face take place

00:00:05.178 --> 00:00:07.505
interpersonally, giving feedback,

00:00:07.505 --> 00:00:11.050
revealing personal information,
apologizing.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:16.267
The key to addressing these tricky
communications issues is to connect and

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:19.513
foster mutually respectful relationships.

00:00:19.513 --> 00:00:23.200
Yet many of us have never learned
how to initiate, build and

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.723
sustain these types of relationships.

00:00:25.723 --> 00:00:27.525
Hello, I'm Matt Abrahams and

00:00:27.525 --> 00:00:31.973
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:31.973 --> 00:00:36.789
Welcome to Think Fast,
Talk Smart, The Podcast.

00:00:36.789 --> 00:00:39.648
To learn more about
the importance of connection, and

00:00:39.648 --> 00:00:42.019
how to foster deep personal relationships.

00:00:42.019 --> 00:00:46.507
I am thrilled to be joined by
Carol Robin and David Bradford,

00:00:46.507 --> 00:00:49.939
who both taught the wildly
popular GSB class,

00:00:49.939 --> 00:00:54.660
interpersonal dynamics,
also known as touchy feely.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.357
Together they have just
written the book Connect,

00:00:57.357 --> 00:01:02.180
Building Exceptional Relationships
with Family, Friends and Colleagues.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.624
Thank you, Carol and David for being here.

00:01:04.624 --> 00:01:05.621
&gt;&gt; I like being here.

00:01:05.621 --> 00:01:06.735
&gt;&gt; For having us.

00:01:06.735 --> 00:01:09.910
&gt;&gt; First off, congrats on your book.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:11.979
I really enjoyed reading it.

00:01:11.979 --> 00:01:16.741
I assess the value of a book by how many
clear takeaways I can immediately put

00:01:16.741 --> 00:01:21.210
into practice and by that measure
your book was a huge success.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:22.510
Shall we get started?

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:23.140
&gt;&gt; Let's go.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.575
&gt;&gt; Absolutely
&gt;&gt; All right, so, Carol,

00:01:25.575 --> 00:01:30.110
in your book you and David talk
about exceptional relationships.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:34.698
What does this mean and what advice do you
have to help people develop exceptional

00:01:34.698 --> 00:01:37.330
relationships in business?

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:41.965
&gt;&gt; An exceptional relationship is one in
which both parties can feel be vulnerable

00:01:41.965 --> 00:01:45.310
with each other and
more fully known by each other.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:47.060
They can be honest with each other and

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.440
trust that their disclosures
will not be used against them.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.839
They can deal with conflict productively.

00:01:52.839 --> 00:01:55.163
They both are committed and

00:01:55.163 --> 00:02:00.831
remain committed to their each
other's growth and development.

00:02:00.831 --> 00:02:05.552
And it's the presence of the combination
of those things that makes a relationship

00:02:05.552 --> 00:02:06.434
exceptional.

00:02:06.434 --> 00:02:09.366
&gt;&gt; What are the key a few key takeaways
you think that can help us build

00:02:09.366 --> 00:02:11.140
these relationships?

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:15.927
&gt;&gt; So the first key, is to recognize that
if you're going to have a relationship

00:02:15.927 --> 00:02:17.695
like we're talking about,

00:02:17.695 --> 00:02:23.010
you have to build the capacity to pick
up signals from two different antenna.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:26.717
One antenna is what's going on for
me internally, the other antennas,

00:02:26.717 --> 00:02:28.552
what's going on for someone else?

00:02:28.552 --> 00:02:33.027
And the more those signals are,
the more you're attuned to those signals.

00:02:33.027 --> 00:02:38.053
And the more they inform the choices you
make in your interaction with someone,

00:02:38.053 --> 00:02:41.438
the more likely you are to
move towards exceptional.

00:02:41.438 --> 00:02:45.930
I'm going to really quickly say,
there's three other keys.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.405
One is take the risk of
allowing yourself to be known,

00:02:48.405 --> 00:02:51.440
because the other person will be
more likely to do the same thing.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:55.001
Be prepared to update your beliefs and

00:02:55.001 --> 00:02:59.294
assumptions about what makes you
effective, particularly as a leader.

00:02:59.294 --> 00:03:02.299
And treat every interaction
as a learning opportunity.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.802
&gt;&gt; I really liked that last point.

00:03:05.802 --> 00:03:07.445
We can learn so much and yet

00:03:07.445 --> 00:03:12.011
we tend to get distracted by just
focusing on what we want to accomplish.

00:03:12.011 --> 00:03:16.054
That we don't take the opportunity to
really see each opportunity as something

00:03:16.054 --> 00:03:17.032
we can learn from.

00:03:17.032 --> 00:03:21.974
And that goes to the first point you made
about thinking about not just what going

00:03:21.974 --> 00:03:22.721
on for you.

00:03:22.721 --> 00:03:25.121
But also think about what's going on for
the other person or

00:03:25.121 --> 00:03:26.330
people you're talking to.

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:31.110
So, thank you for that advice, I think
that's very clear and very helpful.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.356
David, one theme in
the classes you've taught and

00:03:34.356 --> 00:03:37.543
in your book,
is the importance of agency and choice.

00:03:37.543 --> 00:03:41.650
Why is this so
important in building relationships?

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.880
&gt;&gt; It's actually very crucial for
a couple of reasons.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:49.661
One is,
think of the difference between somebody

00:03:49.661 --> 00:03:54.612
saying they're talking about
a relationship with a friend, and

00:03:54.612 --> 00:03:59.710
they say, I can't raise it,
I choose not to raise it.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:00.950
So a world of difference.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:05.650
First, I'm disempowering myself,
I'm helpless,

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:10.330
I'm controlled by the environment or
by the other person.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:13.701
The second person, the second reason is,

00:04:13.701 --> 00:04:19.290
I'm owning that I have agency,
I'm an active participant.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:24.200
Now I may not choose to raise that but
it is a choice.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:25.594
So whenever the students or

00:04:25.594 --> 00:04:28.328
even our friends because
we drive our friends crazy.

00:04:28.328 --> 00:04:30.981
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And we come in say, no,

00:04:30.981 --> 00:04:33.720
you're choosing not to do that.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:37.458
You may want to not do it,
but it's a choice.

00:04:37.458 --> 00:04:43.492
Now the second reason why this is
important is if I owned it, it's a choice.

00:04:43.492 --> 00:04:45.570
It gets me into further exploration.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:52.202
I choose not to raise this
point with Charlie, wonder why?

00:04:52.202 --> 00:04:55.638
It's a Charlie, is it me?

00:04:55.638 --> 00:05:00.630
Do I need his approval,
what am I concerned about?

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:04.693
And that in essence is both
a source of learning but

00:05:04.693 --> 00:05:07.900
it's also gives you further choices.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:15.820
As, for example, I may be afraid
that he's going to deny it.

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:20.172
All that gets me to thinking about
how I'm going to raise it and

00:05:20.172 --> 00:05:21.513
also how I might respond.

00:05:21.513 --> 00:05:27.446
So really owning the fact that
I have choice gives me freedom,

00:05:27.446 --> 00:05:32.936
empowers me,
makes me an active learning participant.

00:05:32.936 --> 00:05:37.825
&gt;&gt; So in essence giving yourself
permission to feel that agency allows you

00:05:37.825 --> 00:05:41.816
so much more freedom in
the relationship and to reflect and

00:05:41.816 --> 00:05:43.866
to motivate yourself to act.

00:05:43.866 --> 00:05:48.256
And I can clearly see how that would help
if you simply feel like you can't do

00:05:48.256 --> 00:05:50.626
something and you don't have control,

00:05:50.626 --> 00:05:54.030
that's going to change
the dynamic completely.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:54.530
&gt;&gt; Totally, totally.

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:57.392
&gt;&gt; You both advocate for

00:05:57.392 --> 00:06:01.983
the importance of disclosure in
building stronger relationships.

00:06:01.983 --> 00:06:06.303
Are there downsides to letting
yourself be known, being honest and

00:06:06.303 --> 00:06:08.370
raising disengagements?

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:13.175
Can one of you share some best practices
that can help us disclose in a productive

00:06:13.175 --> 00:06:14.260
and safe manner?

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.708
&gt;&gt; Take a shot at the first
half of that question and

00:06:18.708 --> 00:06:23.900
then maybe ask David if he'd like to offer
up some tips around the second half.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:24.610
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.297
&gt;&gt; I would argue there's
a bigger downside for

00:06:28.297 --> 00:06:33.190
not allowing yourself to be known for
a number of reasons.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:37.000
First of all,
human beings like to make sense of things.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.545
And the less I tell you about me,

00:06:39.545 --> 00:06:44.270
the more opportunity I give you
to make up stories about me.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:48.603
So, second of all,
if I don't tell you much about me,

00:06:48.603 --> 00:06:53.200
you're less likely to
tell me much about you.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:57.058
And to the extent that moving towards
an exceptional relationship requires

00:06:57.058 --> 00:07:00.067
both of us to be willing to allow
ourselves to be more known,

00:07:00.067 --> 00:07:02.990
we're moving in the wrong direction.

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:07.890
And third, I'd say that the downside
of not being honest and

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:14.246
not raising disagreements is you have
more dysfunction in the relationship.

00:07:14.246 --> 00:07:19.900
So for best practices,
maybe David's got a couple suggestions.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:25.078
&gt;&gt; But what I would build on what
you're saying, Carol, very nicely said,

00:07:25.078 --> 00:07:30.770
is to indulge that matters right, there
is a risk here, there's always a risk.

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:36.513
So in your question,
you said are there safe ways?

00:07:36.513 --> 00:07:38.960
There's nothing that's completely safe.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:45.910
What one can do is, lower the probability
of it going south or is a risk.

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:48.171
And this is why we stress in the book and

00:07:48.171 --> 00:07:53.780
we stress in the class the willingness to
take a risk because that's how you learn.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:57.980
But I think one of the ways to look
at it is we talk about the 15% rule.

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:02.890
And I want you to think of
three concentric circles.

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:08.646
The inner one, smallest one is my
comfort zone where I can operate and

00:08:08.646 --> 00:08:11.230
feel perfectly safe.

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:13.330
But I'm not taking any risk.

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:16.613
And we urge people to take a 15% risk,

00:08:16.613 --> 00:08:21.280
which is the next ring
around this center one.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:24.175
Well, I'm not sharing everything, but

00:08:24.175 --> 00:08:28.570
I'm 15% out of my comfort zone and
a little uncomfortable.

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:34.060
Now, if this doesn't go well,
it's probably not a disaster.

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:36.690
But in all likelihood,
it's going to go well.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:42.260
But it's only 15%, I don't move to
the third ring, which is a dangerous zone.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:46.132
But if my 15% works well as Carol says,

00:08:46.132 --> 00:08:51.810
you might share 15%,
that I might share another 15%.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:56.395
So it's a gradual building
process where we find out what is

00:08:56.395 --> 00:08:58.650
working with each other.

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:04.460
We're both taking some risks but we're
not threatening the entire relationship.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:06.770
&gt;&gt; I like that.

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:09.890
So it sounds like there's
reciprocal risk taking.

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:14.998
And in many ways, I like the concentric
circle model because I think at least for

00:09:14.998 --> 00:09:18.130
me, I often think about
disclosure as binary.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:20.609
Either I disclose or I don't.

00:09:20.609 --> 00:09:26.236
But what I hear you saying is that there
are choices you can make that allow you to

00:09:26.236 --> 00:09:31.791
disclose some and then in essence
an experiment, a test to see the response.

00:09:31.791 --> 00:09:36.300
And I like that, and it feeds back to your
previous response David about agency.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:37.911
It makes you feel in control,

00:09:37.911 --> 00:09:41.850
because sometimes disclosing you feel
like you're totally out of control.

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:44.000
So I like that a lot.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.043
&gt;&gt; But if I could add something.

00:09:46.043 --> 00:09:48.735
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; It ties in to your point about when we

00:09:48.735 --> 00:09:53.339
think of disclosing, we often think
of disclosing is the way I put it,

00:09:53.339 --> 00:09:58.040
something illegal, immoral, or
in fact what we've done in the past.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:01.559
And what we find in the course,

00:10:01.559 --> 00:10:07.438
is that disclosing feelings is
the most powerful way to communicate.

00:10:07.438 --> 00:10:08.260
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:11.800
&gt;&gt; It communicates us thoughts and
feelings.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:15.240
Am I feeling uncomfortable?

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.519
Am I feeling worried about where we are?

00:10:18.519 --> 00:10:23.135
Those are often the 15% risk
that builds the relationship

00:10:23.135 --> 00:10:26.403
because you know what's important to me.

00:10:26.403 --> 00:10:31.141
&gt;&gt; And I would add there's a reason
the students call the course touchy-feely.

00:10:31.141 --> 00:10:35.103
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because the importance of feelings in

00:10:35.103 --> 00:10:40.563
communication is underscored and
highlighted For an entire quarter,

00:10:40.563 --> 00:10:45.568
including the fact that they all
receive a vocabulary of feelings

00:10:45.568 --> 00:10:51.484
as part of the syllabus and by the way,
it's an appendix in the book Content.

00:10:53.085 --> 00:10:57.423
&gt;&gt; That's great, because I think a lot of
us might resonate with what you just said

00:10:57.423 --> 00:11:01.715
about sharing feelings, but might not
have the language or the tools to do it.

00:11:01.715 --> 00:11:04.508
So it's great that there's
a guide that can help.

00:11:04.508 --> 00:11:09.178
So Carol, one of the first times I heard
you speak was on the topic of feedback and

00:11:09.178 --> 00:11:13.910
I have to tell you, I was just totally
transfixed by what you were saying.

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:17.918
And in that conversation,
you stress the value of feedback and

00:11:17.918 --> 00:11:23.200
said that feedback is a gift yet
most people resist giving or receiving it.

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:24.320
Why is that?

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:28.038
And what can people do to get
better about their feedback?

00:11:28.038 --> 00:11:32.981
&gt;&gt; Well, this is going to tie
in a moment to the feelings

00:11:32.981 --> 00:11:37.703
we were talking about,
but let me start by why.

00:11:37.703 --> 00:11:41.067
&gt;&gt; Most people have experienced
stepping in a pile of doo doo when

00:11:41.067 --> 00:11:43.590
they either tried to
give somebody feedback,

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:48.760
or somebody tried to give them feedback,
they observed a feedback exchange.

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:53.657
And so what happens is David likes to say
a cat never sits on a hot stove twice, but

00:11:53.657 --> 00:11:57.180
it never sits in a cold
stove again either.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:01.629
So what happens is we
don't get better by not

00:12:01.629 --> 00:12:07.470
giving feedback,
we don't develop more skill.

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:10.312
And this mental model we hold,
this assumption and

00:12:10.312 --> 00:12:13.023
belief that it's going to
harm the relationship,

00:12:13.023 --> 00:12:16.700
something is going to go terribly awry,
gets reinforced.

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:19.880
It's as if we have no new
data to update it with.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:23.949
So we hold these beliefs and by the way,
one of the reasons the course is so

00:12:23.949 --> 00:12:28.491
powerful and thousands of alums for
decades have found it so transformational,

00:12:28.491 --> 00:12:32.432
is that they got an opportunity to
experiment with giving feedback and

00:12:32.432 --> 00:12:35.730
updating these mental
models that they hold.

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:38.370
And have discovered that it
actually builds relationships,

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:40.570
it doesn't ruin relationships.

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:45.291
Now, one way you're more likely to
move into experimentation is with

00:12:45.291 --> 00:12:47.650
a little more skill.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:52.149
So a big part of the book is dedicated
to some of the skills that you need

00:12:52.149 --> 00:12:56.370
in order to be more effective
in giving feedback.

00:12:56.370 --> 00:13:00.840
There's a central model to the course and
the book called the net.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:05.773
And that's a very specific way of
giving somebody feedback that's

00:13:05.773 --> 00:13:10.534
both behaviorally specific, and
includes the reaction of other

00:13:10.534 --> 00:13:15.590
person's behaviors,
your reactions to their behaviors.

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:19.609
So for example, my husband comes
home this is now many years ago,

00:13:19.609 --> 00:13:21.155
the stories in the book.

00:13:21.155 --> 00:13:24.120
Many years ago, he comes home
from a long day in the valley.

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:27.851
He's been working very hard, he collapses
in the chair in the front room and

00:13:27.851 --> 00:13:29.110
grabs the newspaper.

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:31.120
I hear him,
I come running out of the bathroom.

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:34.110
And I start talking, my God, you're home.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:36.295
Thank God blah, blah,
blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:13:36.295 --> 00:13:41.370
And he does not raise his
eyes from his newspaper.

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:45.117
And the only thing he responds with is.

00:13:45.117 --> 00:13:47.820
Now that's the behavior,
no eye contact and grunk.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:55.654
My reaction is I feel dismissed and
I don't feel heard and

00:13:55.654 --> 00:14:01.007
I feel hurt, but
appears the problem in an exchange

00:14:01.007 --> 00:14:06.000
between two people there
are three realities.

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:12.560
There's reality number one,
what's going on for Andy.

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:16.166
Reality number number two,
the behavior he's engaging in.

00:14:16.166 --> 00:14:19.760
The only reality that's
known to both of us.

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:23.080
And there's reality number three,
which is what's happening for me.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:29.851
And we talk about a metaphorical
net between what's going on for

00:14:29.851 --> 00:14:34.170
him, his reality number one and
the other two.

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:38.762
So I don't know what's going on for
him, but what I do when I don't

00:14:38.762 --> 00:14:43.780
understand the concept of the net is
I say, you're not listening to me.

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:47.270
Well, that assumes that I'm in his head.

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:49.930
And I know and I don't.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:54.420
You're not listening to me is
over the net and it gets worse.

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:56.850
I feel that you don't care.

00:14:56.850 --> 00:14:58.970
First of all, is not a feeling.

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:03.650
And second of all is an attribution,
it's imputing a motive.

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:06.330
I don't know whether he cares or
not, unless he says I don't care.

00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:12.126
So until I learned to say,
when you make no eye contact and

00:15:12.126 --> 00:15:16.300
the only thing I get is a grunt,
I don't feel heard.

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:18.150
And I feel hurt and dismissed.

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:22.541
And I'm telling you this because it
makes me less inclined to want to be

00:15:22.541 --> 00:15:24.250
there for you.

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:28.806
That's the complete package of a piece of
feedback that's entirely on my side of

00:15:28.806 --> 00:15:30.120
the net.

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:33.398
And it's likely to make him
less defensive and saying,

00:15:33.398 --> 00:15:38.209
I feel that you're not sensitive or I feel
that you don't care because by the way,

00:15:38.209 --> 00:15:39.970
that's incredibly unfair.

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:41.935
He's the most sensitive man on the planet.

00:15:41.935 --> 00:15:46.756
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] If I could add a little
bit to that which is a great

00:15:46.756 --> 00:15:51.470
description, is we also say
around the GSB [COUGH] And

00:15:51.470 --> 00:15:56.506
this is part of the notion is
that feedback is a gift, and

00:15:56.506 --> 00:16:02.256
it takes two to no one
As Andy needs Carol to know,

00:16:02.256 --> 00:16:06.320
nd he needs to know
the effect of his behavior.

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:11.008
If we don't know the effect of our
behavior, we're shooting in the dark, and

00:16:11.008 --> 00:16:14.220
you don't hit the target
when you're shooting.

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:19.178
So that's why feedback is a gift when it's
given with the intention to help each

00:16:19.178 --> 00:16:22.517
other to help the other and
to help the relationship.

00:16:22.517 --> 00:16:26.378
&gt;&gt; And if we go back to your question
about receiving feedback by the way if you

00:16:26.378 --> 00:16:30.633
want others to be better givers then it
behooves you to become a better receiver.

00:16:30.633 --> 00:16:31.588
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:16:31.588 --> 00:16:36.010
&gt;&gt; Date we say it's a gift and by the way
sometimes it's wrapped in a really ugly

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:39.343
wrapping really hard to tell
there's even a gift right.

00:16:39.343 --> 00:16:42.913
But if you can respond to a piece of
feedback even if its over the net because

00:16:42.913 --> 00:16:46.670
the person didn't read the book, and
they don't know what they're doing.

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:50.290
You can respond with respond
with curiosity and inquiry.

00:16:50.290 --> 00:16:55.480
Hah, what is it that I have done, and
what exactly is the result of that?

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:56.780
How is that making you feel?

00:16:56.780 --> 00:17:00.783
You can push them back over the net.

00:17:00.783 --> 00:17:02.621
&gt;&gt; Wow, well thank you both for

00:17:02.621 --> 00:17:06.800
giving us the gift of insight
into how to give better feedback.

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:10.009
From my perspective what I
hear you talking about is,

00:17:10.009 --> 00:17:12.530
it's about the approach you take.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:17.389
It's about understanding all parties
involved in what information

00:17:17.389 --> 00:17:21.241
they have access to and
taking time to be deliberate and

00:17:21.241 --> 00:17:24.437
thoughtful in giving feedback is critical.

00:17:24.437 --> 00:17:29.524
So thank you for that and I for
one have received many gifts that did not

00:17:29.524 --> 00:17:35.130
appear to be gifts at first, but
in hindsight they certainly were.

00:17:35.130 --> 00:17:39.288
I'm curious to get your suggestions for
how we can better connect and

00:17:39.288 --> 00:17:43.960
develop our relationships now that so
much of our communication is virtual.

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:46.080
Do you have some suggestions for us?

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:52.504
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, first of all, everything
that we've been talking about and

00:17:52.504 --> 00:17:54.662
everything that's in the book,

00:17:54.662 --> 00:17:59.820
I think is something to consider
doubling down during this time.

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:04.470
So, the fact is that it's,
I think we've become much more

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:10.236
transactional as a result of the pandemic,
and much more task oriented,

00:18:10.236 --> 00:18:15.220
especially in business at
the expense of relationships.

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:17.831
So we have a lot of contact maybe but

00:18:17.831 --> 00:18:22.433
not a whole lot of connection and
if we want more connection.

00:18:22.433 --> 00:18:27.009
Then we've got to make a little
bit of time to ask each other how

00:18:27.009 --> 00:18:29.920
you really do what's really going on.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:35.057
I have a CEO that's in my program
in my leaders in tech program

00:18:35.057 --> 00:18:39.895
right now who starts every
meeting by having every member

00:18:39.895 --> 00:18:44.654
of his team start with a two
minute if you really knew me.

00:18:44.654 --> 00:18:50.099
And then he times them in for two minutes
they have to complete that phrase.

00:18:50.099 --> 00:18:53.818
&gt;&gt; That distinction between contact and
connection,

00:18:53.818 --> 00:18:56.090
opened up so many doors for me.

00:18:56.090 --> 00:19:00.506
I've really been struggling, because I'm
thinking to myself, I'm talking to so

00:19:00.506 --> 00:19:05.700
many people, and teaching so many people,
and yet I don't feel the same connection.

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:09.200
Than I used to, and thank you for
helping make that distinction.

00:19:09.200 --> 00:19:13.823
So we have to take the time to actually
connect it doesn't mean that we're looking

00:19:13.823 --> 00:19:18.310
at faces on a screen, you actually
have to connect with those faces.

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:18.810
Thank you.

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:23.610
Before we end, I'd like to ask
you the same three questions.

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:26.704
I asked everyone who joins
me are you guys up for that?

00:19:26.704 --> 00:19:27.788
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sounds good.

00:19:27.788 --> 00:19:28.630
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:29.170
&gt;&gt; Excellent.

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:35.190
So Carol, if you were to capture the best
communication advice you ever received

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:40.430
as a five to seven word presentation,
slide title, what would it be?

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:45.834
&gt;&gt; Chairman whether this really
comes down to five words.

00:19:45.834 --> 00:19:47.390
&gt;&gt; It's okay you can have a few extra.

00:19:48.766 --> 00:19:57.590
Interpersonal success depends on who
you're talking to, and for what purpose.

00:19:57.590 --> 00:19:58.760
&gt;&gt; Okay, that's a little extra, but

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:00.760
we're going to let it slide
because it's really insightful.

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:04.524
Tell me a little bit more.

00:20:04.524 --> 00:20:08.709
&gt;&gt; So, one of the biggest mistakes people
make is they think one size fits all.

00:20:09.850 --> 00:20:12.913
One of the most important things
students learn in touchy feely

00:20:12.913 --> 00:20:16.435
is the exact opposite, which is why we
were going to write five easy steps

00:20:16.435 --> 00:20:18.420
to be more interpersonally effective.

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:26.303
The fact is,
connecting with someone else is nuanced.

00:20:26.303 --> 00:20:29.880
It's idiosyncratic to that
particular relationship.

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:34.084
What works for you and
me Matt to connect more deeply may or

00:20:34.084 --> 00:20:37.150
may not be what works for
David and me and so

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:41.806
to the extent that I want to be
effective in connecting with you.

00:20:41.806 --> 00:20:46.562
I've got to take you and me in our
relationship into consideration in

00:20:46.562 --> 00:20:50.890
the context of the relationship,
are we work colleagues?

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:52.310
Are we friends?

00:20:52.310 --> 00:20:56.955
And so I think that's kind of
what's underneath the seven word or

00:20:56.955 --> 00:21:00.062
at nine words or
however many words I have.

00:21:00.062 --> 00:21:00.590
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:04.091
Well, Carol, it is a true pleasure to
call to have you as a work colleague and

00:21:04.091 --> 00:21:04.600
a friend.

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:06.010
So thank you.

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:09.757
Who is a communicator that you admire and
why?

00:21:09.757 --> 00:21:12.530
&gt;&gt; I'm going to pick two people I can-
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:17.720
&gt;&gt; First person is a therapist
I had many years ago.

00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:23.637
And Lawrence was so effective because.

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:28.164
I sense that she really
wanted to get to know me.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:35.404
One thing she was very honest and
she pulled no punches,

00:21:35.404 --> 00:21:39.889
but she was also aware of
as a therapist would say.

00:21:39.889 --> 00:21:43.052
When their shit gets in the way, and

00:21:43.052 --> 00:21:48.113
when she was hooked by something I say,
she would stop and

00:21:48.113 --> 00:21:53.510
say "oop that's me, I'm sorry and
it was just wonderful.

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:58.870
The other person I'm going to
name actually is Carol.

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:05.760
And we've tested the communication now for
many many years but

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:10.650
over the last 3.5 years I'm
going to say as to Carol.

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:15.728
Carol why I pick you is most of
the time you're clean in terms

00:22:15.728 --> 00:22:20.909
of what you say, and
second when you're not I can raise it and

00:22:20.909 --> 00:22:27.450
I rarely experience you getting
defensive or explain yourself.

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:32.127
But you really do live with a notion
that feedback is a gift and

00:22:32.127 --> 00:22:37.120
you're doing something and
we clean it up right away.

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:40.700
And I find after we have cleaned it up,
I feel closer to you.

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:42.105
It is built the relationship.

00:22:42.105 --> 00:22:47.352
So I mean that and I have really valued

00:22:47.352 --> 00:22:53.624
you as a co author and
as a very close friend.

00:22:53.624 --> 00:22:54.360
&gt;&gt; Thank you David.

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:59.431
I'm really touched and right back at

00:22:59.431 --> 00:23:05.000
you in every regard, so who do I admire?

00:23:06.670 --> 00:23:08.675
The first person that sprang to mind for

00:23:08.675 --> 00:23:11.060
me that's obviously
famous is Barack Obama.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:17.804
And the reason obviously,
he's a great orator and he's very,

00:23:17.804 --> 00:23:22.909
his very succinct and articulate and
just a pleasure to listen to.

00:23:22.909 --> 00:23:28.010
But the reason he came to mind for me was
two and I've had the immense privilege and

00:23:28.010 --> 00:23:31.284
pleasure of meeting him in person,
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:23:31.284 --> 00:23:35.252
&gt;&gt; Is that what you see
is what you get and

00:23:35.252 --> 00:23:40.116
of course he's got his public persona and
but

00:23:40.116 --> 00:23:44.030
I feel incredibly drawn to him.

00:23:44.030 --> 00:23:48.820
And the reason I feel drawn to him is
he's not afraid to show his emotions.

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:52.625
He's not afraid to talk
about how he's feeling.

00:23:52.625 --> 00:23:55.274
He has conviction and strength, but

00:23:55.274 --> 00:23:59.940
also of vulnerability in
a willingness to be wrong.

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:01.320
That is inspiring.

00:24:03.280 --> 00:24:06.985
&gt;&gt; I totally agree I've had the great
fortune not to meet him but

00:24:06.985 --> 00:24:11.850
I met one of his speech writers who
echoes everything that you just said.

00:24:11.850 --> 00:24:13.320
So thank you both for sharing that.

00:24:14.990 --> 00:24:19.626
So David, what are the first
three ingredients that go into

00:24:19.626 --> 00:24:24.364
a successful communication
recipe from your perspective?

00:24:24.364 --> 00:24:29.390
&gt;&gt; Well I'm Carol stole my thunder but
that's

00:24:29.390 --> 00:24:30.518
&gt;&gt; No am sorry [LAUGH]

00:24:30.518 --> 00:24:33.671
&gt;&gt; No it's wonderful because I'm going to

00:24:33.671 --> 00:24:36.738
build it it's really very important.

00:24:36.738 --> 00:24:40.540
We sometimes communicate
to get the task done but

00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:47.340
I want to talk about the interplay
between communication and relationships.

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:53.627
First, first thing is that love
the three that you asked for

00:24:53.627 --> 00:24:58.783
is I have to take account
of this relationship and

00:24:58.783 --> 00:25:04.092
not treat relationships
as a general statement.

00:25:04.092 --> 00:25:08.097
&gt;&gt; What is the other words
the other person right now,

00:25:08.097 --> 00:25:13.236
what do they need, what do I need
how robust is our relationship.

00:25:13.236 --> 00:25:18.242
So that's the first private I
take how the relationship then I

00:25:18.242 --> 00:25:23.535
need to take account of the fact
that in most of my conversations,

00:25:23.535 --> 00:25:29.350
I want to communicate in order
to build this relationship.

00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:35.502
And this isn't just with intimate
people that I have, but I find, for

00:25:35.502 --> 00:25:41.690
example, when I go to the store,
I do the shopping for the household.

00:25:41.690 --> 00:25:45.004
&gt;&gt; I like to have a more
personal relationship,

00:25:45.004 --> 00:25:48.317
even with the clerk and
could I communicate in

00:25:48.317 --> 00:25:53.620
a way that shows that person I
see that person as an individual?

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:57.280
Solely of two minute interaction.

00:25:57.280 --> 00:26:01.450
So I want to see communication as
a way to build the relationship.

00:26:02.530 --> 00:26:05.610
And the third one is,
if I have done that and

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:11.780
I have built a strong relationship,
I can communicate in so many more ways.

00:26:11.780 --> 00:26:14.780
I can share so much more about myself.

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:17.641
I can even make more
mistakes because I know

00:26:17.641 --> 00:26:20.511
we have the foundation to recover from it.

00:26:20.511 --> 00:26:26.916
So for me the communication has to be
closely tied with the relationship

00:26:26.916 --> 00:26:32.056
they're intertwined and
they have to be seen together.

00:26:32.056 --> 00:26:32.987
&gt;&gt; Very powerful and

00:26:32.987 --> 00:26:36.760
important point that communication
happens within a relationship.

00:26:36.760 --> 00:26:40.930
And I like how you layer that description
where you start with appreciating

00:26:40.930 --> 00:26:43.644
the relationship building
the relationship and

00:26:43.644 --> 00:26:47.571
that frees you to have a variety of
different types of communication.

00:26:47.571 --> 00:26:52.230
&gt;&gt; Thank you both your insights and
ideas have been incredibly helpful.

00:26:52.230 --> 00:26:57.542
To my mind, they boil down to agency,
introspection and connection,

00:26:57.542 --> 00:27:01.979
and especially balancing out
both thoughts and feelings.

